summary:
- topic: Grievance Vote
  text: Following an executive session, the board began the public meeting by voting
    5-2 to deny a grievance and directed the superintendent to provide written notice
    of the decision.
- topic: Recognitions
  text: A significant portion of the meeting was dedicated to recognitions, honoring
    music teacher Craig Skeffington for his induction into the Maine Music Educators
    Association Hall of Fame, the SPHS Robotics Team for a $5,000 grant from Consolidated
    Communications, and the SPHS Boys’ Track Team for their Class A State Championship.
- topic: Community Partnerships Report
  text: The administration presented a detailed report on Community Partnerships,
    highlighting the contributions of nearly 500 volunteers, numerous donations for
    student needs, and various career exploration programs like ELOs and partnerships
    with local businesses.
- topic: Federal Funding Update
  text: Dr. Prince provided an update on Federal Title Funding (ESEA), reporting a
    significant decrease of nearly $150,000 for the upcoming fiscal year, primarily
    in Title I funds, and outlined the proposed allocation of the remaining funds
    to support students at Kaler and Skillin schools, as well as multilingual learners.
- topic: Facilities Bond Referendum Discussion
  text: The board held a lengthy discussion on a potential facilities bond referendum.
    The Superintendent proposed projects for school security, deferred maintenance,
    and a major renovation of the high school stadium complex, including a controversial
    recommendation for an artificial turf field using specific recyclable and organic-infill
    products.
- topic: Facilities Bond Resolution
  text: After debate on the environmental and safety aspects of artificial turf versus
    natural grass, the board unanimously voted to direct the administration to prepare
    formal proposals for three separate bond referendums (safety, maintenance, and
    stadium) with a total cost not to exceed $17 million.
